markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Section 6: Beyond the Basics: Sensing and Responsibility</h1>

<div style="display: inline-block; background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #1F1976; font-weight: bold; font-size: 13px; padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 10px; margin-bottom: 12px;">Aligns to curriculum Lessons 10-12</div>

<p>This section prepares you to teach three curriculum lessons back to back. First, how computers sense the world at all, not with eyes or ears, but with numbers. Students reveal a hidden image inside a grid of bits, represent a sound as a set of features in a Python dictionary, and build a classifier that tells two instruments apart.</p><p> Then the section turns to a different question: once a model can sense and predict, how do you tell if it's making the right call? Students evaluate real models for fairness and safety before those terms are ever defined for them. Finally, the section turns from evaluating models after the fact to building them ethically from the start.</p><br>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><b>Curriculum Lesson 10:</b> Sensing the World</div>
  <a href="PASTE_UNIT_2_LESSON_10_URL_HERE" style="display: inline-block; background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; padding: 6px 14px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; margin-top: 6px; font-size: 13px;">View Curriculum Lesson 10</a>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><b>Curriculum Lesson 11:</b> Evaluating the Impact of Models</div>
  <a href="PASTE_UNIT_2_LESSON_11_URL_HERE" style="display: inline-block; background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; padding: 6px 14px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; margin-top: 6px; font-size: 13px;">View Curriculum Lesson 11</a>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><b>Curriculum Lesson 12:</b> Building Models Ethically</div>
  <a href="PASTE_UNIT_2_LESSON_12_URL_HERE" style="display: inline-block; background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; padding: 6px 14px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; margin-top: 6px; font-size: 13px;">View Curriculum Lesson 12</a>
</div>

<hr>

<p><b>In this section, you'll:</b></p>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; See how a computer represents an image as a grid of bits, and reveal a hidden image the same way your students will</div>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; Represent a sound as a set of features, frequency, volume, brightness, bundled in a Python dictionary</div>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; See why two features can both be real and still not be enough to tell two things apart</div>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; Watch how adding a third feature turns a weak classifier into one that gets 90%+ of notes right</div>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; Spot machine learning hiding in everyday scenarios, and name what it's actually predicting</div>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; Compare real models side by side to see why the most accurate one isn't always the right one</div>
<div style="font-size: 16px;">&bull; Build the habit of asking fairness and safety questions while a model is still being designed, not just after it's deployed</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><b>&#128161; Notice as you explore:</b> this section builds in three stages. First, representation, a computer only sees what you choose to measure, the same idea that showed up back in Section 4 and Section 5 as feature selection. Then evaluation, once a model is sensing and predicting, what does it mean for it to get things right? Finally, responsibility, taking what you just learned to judge a model and using it to build one well in the first place.</div>
</div>

<hr>

